--TEST--
a bare relative path is resolved against the current directory before it is compared to a grant, so `.` covers the file a program is about to create — `rule:security/capability-question-is-grant-and-scope` and `rule:security/capability-check-at-the-door`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A bare name has no parent to canonicalise -- `Path::parent` of `missing.txt`
// is `""` -- so the argument side of the comparison has to reach the current
// directory on its own, or a grant of `.` covers nothing a program actually
// spells. Catching `IOError` first names which half of the door ran, exactly as
// `file-asks-for-the-capability-before-it-touches-the-path.nvst` does: the
// capability is granted here, so the operating system is what has to answer.
try {
    string $missing = Core\IO::read("missing.txt");
    echo "read missing.txt: opened\n";
} catch (IOError $touched) {
    echo "read missing.txt: the os answered first\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo "read missing.txt: refused before the os\n";
}

// A write creates the path it names, so there is never anything to canonicalise
// and the deepest existing ancestor *is* the current directory. This is the
// spelling `write = ["."]` exists for.
Core\IO::write("copy.txt", "copied\n");
echo Core\IO::read("copy.txt");

// The bound on the other side: resolving a bare name against the current
// directory is not resolving every relative name into the grant. One that
// climbs out of it lands outside `.` and is still refused.
try {
    Core\IO::write("../escape.txt", "x");
    echo "write ../escape.txt: created\n";
} catch (IOError $reached) {
    echo "write ../escape.txt: the os answered first\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $denied) {
    echo "write ../escape.txt: refused\n";
}
--FILE nvs.toml--
[capabilities.fs]
read = ["."]
write = ["."]
--EXPECT--
read missing.txt: the os answered first
copied
write ../escape.txt: refused
